WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.880 --> 00:09.250
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

00:11.610 --> 00:13.050
Good afternoon.

00:13.050 --> 00:18.260
It's been about three and a half years since the economic recovery began.

00:18.260 --> 00:21.640
The economy continues to expand at a moderate pace.

00:21.640 --> 00:24.820
Unfortunately, however, unemployment remains high.

00:24.820 --> 00:28.610
About 5 million people-more than 40 percent of the unemployed-have been

00:28.610 --> 00:32.600
without a job for six months or more, and millions more who say they would

00:32.600 --> 00:36.550
like full-time work have been able to find only part-time employment

00:36.550 --> 00:39.410
or have stopped looking entirely.

00:39.410 --> 00:43.130
The conditions now prevailing in the job market represent an enormous waste

00:43.130 --> 00:46.150
of human and economic potential.

00:46.150 --> 00:49.790
A return to broad-based prosperity will require sustained improvement

00:49.790 --> 00:55.090
in the job market, which in turn requires stronger economic growth.

00:55.090 --> 00:59.220
Meanwhile, apart from some temporary fluctuations that largely reflected swings

00:59.220 --> 01:03.770
in energy prices, inflation has remained tame and appears likely to run at

01:03.770 --> 01:07.020
or below the Federal Open Market Committee's (FOMC's) 2 percent objective

01:07.020 --> 01:11.430
in coming quarters and over the longer term.

01:11.430 --> 01:14.690
Against a macroeconomic backdrop that includes both high unemployment

01:14.690 --> 01:20.580
and subdued inflation, the FOMC will maintain its highly accommodative policy.

01:20.580 --> 01:23.340
Today the Committee took several steps.

01:23.340 --> 01:25.740
First, it decided to continue its purchases

01:25.740 --> 01:30.100
of agency mortgage-backed securities (MBS), initiated at the September meeting,

01:30.100 --> 01:33.720
at a pace of $40 billion per month.

01:33.720 --> 01:37.160
Second, the Committee decided to purchase longer-term Treasury securities,

01:37.160 --> 01:40.450
initially at a pace of $45 billion per month,

01:40.450 --> 01:43.200
after its current program to extend the average maturity

01:43.200 --> 01:46.350
of its holdings is completed at the end of the year.

01:46.350 --> 01:48.940
In continuing its asset purchases,

01:48.940 --> 01:52.880
the Committee seeks to maintain downward pressure on longer-term interest rates

01:52.880 --> 01:56.610
and to keep financial conditions accommodative, thereby promoting hiring

01:56.610 --> 02:00.870
and economic growth while ensuring that inflation over time is close

02:00.870 --> 02:03.470
to our 2 percent objective.

02:03.470 --> 02:08.120
Finally, the Committee today also modified its guidance about future rate policy

02:08.120 --> 02:11.870
to provide more information to the public about how it anticipates it will react

02:11.870 --> 02:14.420
to evolving economic conditions.

02:14.420 --> 02:17.750
I will return to this change in our communication after discussing our decision

02:17.750 --> 02:19.560
to continue asset purchases.

02:19.560 --> 02:24.600
Although the Committee's announcement today specified the initial monthly pace

02:24.600 --> 02:26.750
and composition of asset purchases,

02:26.750 --> 02:31.490
it did not give specific dates at which the program may be modified or ended.

02:31.490 --> 02:34.350
Instead, the pattern of future asset purchases will depend

02:34.350 --> 02:39.680
on the Committee's evaluation of incoming information, in two respects.

02:39.680 --> 02:42.520
First, we expect to continue asset purchases

02:42.520 --> 02:45.970
until we see a substantial improvement in the outlook for the labor market,

02:45.970 --> 02:48.820
in a context of price stability.

02:48.820 --> 02:52.400
In assessing the extent of progress, the Committee will be evaluating a range

02:52.400 --> 02:56.880
of labor market indicators, including the unemployment rate, payroll employment,

02:56.880 --> 03:01.210
hours worked, and labor force participation, among others.

03:01.210 --> 03:04.510
Because increases in demand and production are normally precursors

03:04.510 --> 03:08.150
to improvements in labor market conditions, we will also be looking carefully

03:08.150 --> 03:10.660
at the pace of economic activity more broadly.

03:10.660 --> 03:15.790
Second, the Committee will be monitoring economic and financial developments

03:15.790 --> 03:19.340
to assess both the efficacy and possible drawbacks

03:19.340 --> 03:21.440
of its asset purchase program.

03:21.440 --> 03:23.550
The Federal Reserve's asset purchases

03:23.550 --> 03:27.390
over the past few years have provided important support to the economy,

03:27.390 --> 03:30.930
for example, by helping to keep mortgage rates historically low.

03:30.930 --> 03:35.480
The Committee expects this policy tool to continue to be effective and the costs

03:35.480 --> 03:39.450
and risks to remain manageable, but as the program continues,

03:39.450 --> 03:42.530
we will be regularly updating those assessments.

03:42.530 --> 03:47.360
If future evidence suggests that the program's effectiveness has declined,

03:47.360 --> 03:51.210
or if potential unintended side effects or risks become apparent

03:51.210 --> 03:56.410
as the balance sheet grows, we will modify the program as appropriate.

03:56.410 --> 03:59.960
More generally, the Committee intends to be flexible in varying the pace

03:59.960 --> 04:03.800
of securities purchases in response to information bearing on the outlook

04:03.800 --> 04:07.020
or on the perceived benefits and costs of the program.

04:07.020 --> 04:11.760
Unlike the explicitly quantitative criteria associated

04:11.760 --> 04:14.490
with the Committee's forward guidance about the federal funds rate,

04:14.490 --> 04:17.810
which I will discuss in a moment, the criteria the Committee will use

04:17.810 --> 04:19.490
to make decisions about the pace

04:19.490 --> 04:24.120
and extent of its asset purchase program are qualitative; in particular,

04:24.120 --> 04:28.390
continuation of asset purchases is tied to our seeing substantial improvement

04:28.390 --> 04:31.110
in the outlook for the labor market.

04:31.110 --> 04:35.130
Because we expect to learn more over time about the efficacy and potential costs

04:35.130 --> 04:38.560
of asset purchases in the current economic context,

04:38.560 --> 04:42.270
we believe that qualitative guidance is more appropriate at this time.

04:42.270 --> 04:47.400
In today's statement, the Committee also recast its forward guidance

04:47.400 --> 04:51.250
to clarify how it expects its target for the federal funds rate to depend

04:51.250 --> 04:53.910
on future economic developments.

04:53.910 --> 04:56.820
Specifically, the Committee anticipates that exceptionally low levels

04:56.820 --> 05:01.350
for the federal funds rate are likely to be warranted "at least as long

05:01.350 --> 05:04.660
as the unemployment rate remains above 6½ percent,

05:04.660 --> 05:07.890
inflation over the period between one and two years ahead is projected

05:07.890 --> 05:10.010
to be no more than half a percentage point

05:10.010 --> 05:13.060
above the Committee's 2 percent longer-run goal,

05:13.060 --> 05:17.230
and longer-term inflation expectations continue to be well anchored."

05:17.230 --> 05:20.800
This formulation is a change from earlier statements in which forward guidance

05:20.800 --> 05:25.560
about the federal funds rate was expressed in terms of a date; for example,

05:25.560 --> 05:28.680
in the statements following its September and October meetings,

05:28.680 --> 05:32.280
the Committee indicated that it anticipated that exceptionally low levels

05:32.280 --> 05:34.840
for the federal funds rate are likely to be warranted

05:34.840 --> 05:38.370
"at least through mid-2015."

05:38.370 --> 05:42.050
The modified formulation makes more explicit the FOMC's intention

05:42.050 --> 05:44.520
to maintain accommodation as long as needed

05:44.520 --> 05:48.810
to promote a stronger economic recovery in the context of price stability,

05:48.810 --> 05:51.520
a strategy that we believe will help support household

05:51.520 --> 05:54.130
and business confidence and spending.

05:54.130 --> 05:59.130
By tying future monetary policy more explicitly to economic conditions,

05:59.130 --> 06:03.160
this formulation of our policy guidance should also make monetary policy more

06:03.160 --> 06:07.270
transparent and predictable to the public.

06:07.270 --> 06:10.200
The change in the form of the Committee's forward guidance does not

06:10.200 --> 06:13.610
in itself imply any change in the Committee's expectations

06:13.610 --> 06:15.290
about the likely future path

06:15.290 --> 06:18.950
of the federal funds rate since the October meeting.

06:18.950 --> 06:21.370
In particular, the Committee expects that the stated threshold

06:21.370 --> 06:24.640
for unemployment will not be reached before mid-2015

06:24.640 --> 06:29.190
and projects that inflation will remain close to 2 percent over that period.

06:29.190 --> 06:31.230
Thus, given the Committee's current outlook,

06:31.230 --> 06:33.900
the guidance introduced today is consistent

06:33.900 --> 06:37.070
with the Committee's earlier statements that "exceptionally low levels

06:37.070 --> 06:39.490
for the federal funds rate are likely to be warranted

06:39.490 --> 06:42.890
at least through mid-2015."

06:42.890 --> 06:45.370
Let me emphasize that the 6½ percent threshold

06:45.370 --> 06:47.900
for the unemployment rate should not be interpreted

06:47.900 --> 06:51.180
as the Committee's longer-term objective for unemployment.

06:51.180 --> 06:54.080
Indeed, in the economic projections submitted in conjunction

06:54.080 --> 06:58.920
with today's meeting, the central tendency of participants' estimates

06:58.920 --> 07:04.750
of the longer-run normal rate of unemployment is 5.2 to 6.0 percent.

07:04.750 --> 07:09.350
However, because changes in monetary policy affect the economy with a lag,

07:09.350 --> 07:11.980
the Committee believes that it likely will need to begin moving away

07:11.980 --> 07:15.800
from a highly accommodative policy stance before the economy reaches

07:15.800 --> 07:17.990
maximum employment.

07:17.990 --> 07:21.060
Waiting until maximum employment is achieved before beginning the process

07:21.060 --> 07:25.170
of removing policy accommodation could lead to an undesirable overshooting

07:25.170 --> 07:29.900
of potential output and compromise the FOMC's longer-term inflation objective

07:29.900 --> 07:31.150
of 2 percent.

07:31.150 --> 07:35.900
As the FOMC statement makes clear, the Committee anticipates that policy

07:35.900 --> 07:39.390
under the new guidance will be fully consistent with continued progress

07:39.390 --> 07:42.720
against unemployment and with inflation remaining close

07:42.720 --> 07:46.280
to the Committee's 2 percent objective over the longer term.

07:46.280 --> 07:50.850
Although the modified guidance should provide greater clarity

07:50.850 --> 07:54.030
about how the Committee expects to respond to incoming data,

07:54.030 --> 07:57.600
it by no means puts monetary policy on autopilot.

07:57.600 --> 07:59.890
In this regard, let me make several points.

07:59.890 --> 08:05.160
First, as the statement notes, the Committee views its current low-rate policy

08:05.160 --> 08:10.620
as likely to be appropriate at least until the specified thresholds are met.

08:10.620 --> 08:12.170
Reaching one of those thresholds, however,

08:12.170 --> 08:16.980
will not automatically trigger immediate reduction in policy accommodation.

08:16.980 --> 08:20.610
For example, if unemployment were to decline to slightly below 6½ percent

08:20.610 --> 08:24.570
at a time when inflation and inflation expectations were subdued

08:24.570 --> 08:28.910
and were projected to remain so, the Committee might judge an immediate increase

08:28.910 --> 08:32.730
in its target for the federal funds rate to be inappropriate.

08:32.730 --> 08:37.110
Ultimately, in deciding when and how quickly to reduce policy accommodation,

08:37.110 --> 08:41.120
the Committee will follow a balanced approach in seeking to mitigate deviations

08:41.120 --> 08:45.760
of inflation from its longer-run 2 percent goal and deviations of employment

08:45.760 --> 08:47.810
from its estimated maximum level.

08:47.810 --> 08:50.970
Second, the Committee recognizes

08:50.970 --> 08:54.710
that no single indicator provides a complete assessment of the state

08:54.710 --> 08:58.340
of the labor market and therefore will consider changes in the unemployment rate

08:58.340 --> 09:01.850
within the broader context of labor market conditions.

09:01.850 --> 09:05.490
For example, in evaluating a given decline in the unemployment rate,

09:05.490 --> 09:07.870
the Committee will also take into account the extent to which

09:07.870 --> 09:12.280
that decline was associated with increases in employment and hours worked,

09:12.280 --> 09:15.960
as opposed to, say, increases in the number of discouraged workers

09:15.960 --> 09:18.980
and falling labor force participation.

09:18.980 --> 09:20.940
The Committee will also consider whether the improvement

09:20.940 --> 09:23.340
in the unemployment rate appears sustainable.

09:23.340 --> 09:28.020
Third, the Committee chose to express the inflation threshold in terms

09:28.020 --> 09:30.980
of projected inflation between one and two years ahead,

09:30.980 --> 09:34.220
rather than in terms of current inflation.

09:34.220 --> 09:37.330
The Committee took this approach to make clear that it intends to look

09:37.330 --> 09:40.830
through purely transitory fluctuations in inflation,

09:40.830 --> 09:44.370
such as those induced by short-term variations in the prices

09:44.370 --> 09:46.540
of internationally traded commodities,

09:46.540 --> 09:50.390
and to focus instead on the underlying inflation trend.

09:50.390 --> 09:55.920
In making its collective judgment about the underlying inflation trend,

09:55.920 --> 09:58.220
the Committee will consider a variety of indicators,

09:58.220 --> 10:02.580
including measures such as median, trimmed mean, and core inflation;

10:02.580 --> 10:06.400
the views of outside forecasters; and the predictions of econometric

10:06.400 --> 10:09.390
and statistical models of inflation.

10:09.390 --> 10:11.660
Also, the Committee will pay close attention to measures

10:11.660 --> 10:13.900
of inflation expectations to ensure

10:13.900 --> 10:17.330
that those expectations remain well anchored.

10:17.330 --> 10:20.810
Finally, the Committee will continue to monitor a wide range of information

10:20.810 --> 10:25.190
on economic and financial developments to ensure that policy is conducted

10:25.190 --> 10:29.040
in a manner consistent with our dual mandate.

10:29.040 --> 10:32.670
It's worth noting that the goals of the FOMC's asset purchases

10:32.670 --> 10:35.970
and of its federal funds rate guidance are somewhat different.

10:35.970 --> 10:39.350
The goal of the asset purchase program is to increase the near-term momentum

10:39.350 --> 10:43.730
of the economy by fostering more-accommodative financial conditions,

10:43.730 --> 10:46.360
while the purpose of the rate guidance is to provide information

10:46.360 --> 10:48.200
about the future circumstances

10:48.200 --> 10:52.280
under which the Committee would contemplate reducing accommodation.

10:52.280 --> 10:55.690
I would emphasize that a decision by the Committee to end asset purchases,

10:55.690 --> 11:00.210
whenever that point is reached, would not be a turn to tighter policy.

11:00.210 --> 11:03.630
While in that circumstance the Committee would no longer be increasing policy

11:03.630 --> 11:09.090
accommodation, its policy stance would remain highly supportive of growth.

11:09.090 --> 11:11.970
Only at some later point would the Committee begin actually removing

11:11.970 --> 11:14.700
accommodation through rate increases.

11:14.700 --> 11:16.160
Moreover, as I have discussed today,

11:16.160 --> 11:18.860
the decisions to modify the asset purchase program

11:18.860 --> 11:22.480
and to undertake rate increases are tied to different criteria.

11:22.480 --> 11:28.600
In conclusion, the FOMC's actions today are part of our ongoing efforts

11:28.600 --> 11:33.470
to support economic recovery and job creation while maintaining price stability.

11:33.470 --> 11:38.410
As I have often stressed, however, monetary policy has its limits;

11:38.410 --> 11:42.650
only the private and public sectors working together can get the U.S. economy

11:42.650 --> 11:44.860
fully back on track.

11:44.860 --> 11:49.340
In particular, it will be critical that fiscal policymakers come together soon

11:49.340 --> 11:54.030
to achieve longer-term fiscal sustainability without adopting policies

11:54.030 --> 11:56.740
that could derail the ongoing recovery.

11:56.740 --> 11:57.070
Thank you.

11:57.070 --> 11:59.610
I would be happy to answer your questions.

11:59.610 --> 12:02.030
STEVE LIESMAN.

12:02.030 --> 12:04.090
Mr. Chairman, Steve Liesman from CNBC.

12:04.090 --> 12:10.830
I guess I have a lot of questions but I'll just offer up two here.

12:10.830 --> 12:16.260
Why are there different targets for QE and for the funds rate?

12:16.260 --> 12:18.850
What does that achieve?

12:18.850 --> 12:22.270
Secondly, what good is a target if you have to make reference to calendar dates

12:22.270 --> 12:24.590
in the statement itself, which is the thing you got away from.

12:24.590 --> 12:27.120
You had to point out in the statement that it's not substantially different

12:27.120 --> 12:28.740
from the calendar dates we set back in October.

12:28.740 --> 12:30.290
Do you have to keep doing that from now

12:30.290 --> 12:32.910
on to make it clear that-and then just thirdly,

12:32.910 --> 12:34.050
I guess I know I said two- CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

12:34.050 --> 12:35.010
I'm going to forget.

12:35.010 --> 12:35.480
STEVE LIESMAN.

12:35.480 --> 12:38.850
But then you have another paragraph after that

12:38.850 --> 12:40.800
that says it's not just target it's something else.

12:40.800 --> 12:43.040
So, it's unclear to me what good these targets are if you have

12:43.040 --> 12:44.190
to reference a calendar date,

12:44.190 --> 12:47.100
and then you kind of say in the next paragraph it's not really targets.

12:47.100 --> 12:48.880
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

12:48.880 --> 12:53.690
Well, so first, the-as I said-the asset purchases

12:53.690 --> 12:57.930
and the rate increases have different objectives.

12:57.930 --> 13:01.500
The asset purchases are about creating some near-term momentum in the economy,

13:01.500 --> 13:05.900
trying to strengthen growth and job creation in the near term,

13:05.900 --> 13:11.470
and the increases in the federal funds rate target, when they ultimately occur,

13:11.470 --> 13:13.580
are about reducing accommodation.

13:13.580 --> 13:15.960
Two very different objectives.

13:15.960 --> 13:25.000
Secondly, the asset purchases are a less well understood tool.

13:25.000 --> 13:29.150
We have-we'll be learning over time about how efficacious they are,

13:29.150 --> 13:34.940
about what costs they may carry with them in terms of unintended consequences

13:34.940 --> 13:39.800
that they might create, and we'll be seeing how-what else happens in the economy

13:39.800 --> 13:43.710
that can affect, you know, the level of unemployment,

13:43.710 --> 13:45.280
for example, that we hope to achieve.

13:45.280 --> 13:48.330
And so, for that reason, as I discussed in my opening remarks,

13:48.330 --> 13:56.450
we decided to make the criteria for asset purchases qualitative at this time

13:56.450 --> 13:58.420
because we have a number of different things that we need

13:58.420 --> 14:00.990
to look at as we go forward.

14:00.990 --> 14:04.280
Rate increases, by contrast, are well understood,

14:04.280 --> 14:09.410
and we understand the relationship between those rate increases

14:09.410 --> 14:12.020
and the state of the economy.

14:12.020 --> 14:14.390
And so we've been able to give somewhat more quantitative,

14:14.390 --> 14:16.660
more specific guidance in that respect.

14:16.660 --> 14:21.800
With respect to the date, in the transition today,

14:21.800 --> 14:26.470
we wanted to make clear that the change in guidance did not-it happens

14:26.470 --> 14:30.490
to be the case that it doesn't change our mid-2015 expectation.

14:30.490 --> 14:38.340
Going forward, we'll drop the date and rely on the conditionality, and that has,

14:38.340 --> 14:41.670
I think, a very important advantage which is that if news comes

14:41.670 --> 14:47.940
in that the economy is stronger or weaker, then financial markets

14:47.940 --> 14:51.040
and the public will be able to adjust their expectations

14:51.040 --> 14:56.010
for when policy tightening will occur without the Committee having to go

14:56.010 --> 15:00.320
through a process of changing its date in a nontransparent way,

15:00.320 --> 15:04.030
so I think that's beneficial.

15:04.030 --> 15:15.630
Does that cover your-okay.

15:15.630 --> 15:16.200
MARCY GORDON.

15:16.200 --> 15:19.880
Mr. Chairman, what prompted the Committee to make the decision

15:19.880 --> 15:23.880
at this particular time to specify targets?

15:23.880 --> 15:30.730
And, by taking an unemployment rate that is quite low compared to currently,

15:30.730 --> 15:36.660
does that shift the balance of priorities in terms of your dual mandate,

15:36.660 --> 15:42.620
closer more in the direction of reducing unemployment rather

15:42.620 --> 15:46.140
than inflationary pressures?

15:46.140 --> 15:46.600
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

15:46.600 --> 15:48.170
That's a very good question.

15:48.170 --> 15:52.490
We took the change today after a good bit of discussion.

15:52.490 --> 15:54.150
We had a very substantial discussion

15:54.150 --> 15:57.580
of the threshold approach at our last meeting.

15:57.580 --> 16:03.800
And we felt it was ready to go, ready to put out.

16:03.800 --> 16:08.690
We-while there are different views on aspects of the threshold approach,

16:08.690 --> 16:13.460
there was a lot of agreement that having a more explicit connection

16:13.460 --> 16:18.390
between rate policy and the state of the economy was more transparent

16:18.390 --> 16:22.950
and more helpful to the markets and to the public than our date-based guidance,

16:22.950 --> 16:27.760
and therefore, there was a general view that, at some point at least,

16:27.760 --> 16:31.640
that we should switch to that kind of guidance.

16:31.640 --> 16:35.180
So we do hope that it'll be more helpful and give markets more information

16:35.180 --> 16:38.330
about how we're going to respond going forward.

16:38.330 --> 16:45.820
It is not a change in our relative balance, weights, towards inflation

16:45.820 --> 16:48.150
and unemployment-by no means.

16:48.150 --> 16:49.840
First of all, with respect to inflation,

16:49.840 --> 16:53.810
we remain completely committed to our 2 percent longer-run objective.

16:53.810 --> 16:57.940
Moreover, we expect our forecast, as you can see from the Summary

16:57.940 --> 17:02.130
of Economic Projections; our forecasts are that inflation will actually remain,

17:02.130 --> 17:08.600
despite this threshold of 2½, that inflation will actually remain at

17:08.600 --> 17:12.650
or below 2 percent going forward.

17:12.650 --> 17:19.370
And so-and finally, the thresholds that we have put out are entirely consistent

17:19.370 --> 17:23.280
with our long-standing views on what the rate path has to be,

17:23.280 --> 17:28.150
what the path of interest rates has to be, in order to achieve improvement

17:28.150 --> 17:31.140
in the labor market while keeping inflation close to target.

17:31.140 --> 17:34.810
So we-I think both sides of the mandate are well-served here.

17:34.810 --> 17:36.390
There is no real change in policy.

17:36.390 --> 17:42.150
What it is instead is an attempt to clarify the relationship

17:42.150 --> 17:44.960
between policy and economic conditions.

17:44.960 --> 17:46.170
MARCY GORDON.

17:46.170 --> 17:52.170
Could I ask, also, given that the-your economic projections are all the more

17:52.170 --> 17:57.200
important now that you've specified these targets,

17:57.200 --> 18:02.220
is it difficult to put forward these projections now given the uncertainty

18:02.220 --> 18:05.050
over the fiscal cliff?

18:05.050 --> 18:09.130
How sort of plastic are these?

18:09.130 --> 18:09.790
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

18:09.790 --> 18:11.620
Are you talking about the SEP projections?

18:11.620 --> 18:13.180
MARCY GORDON.

18:13.180 --> 18:14.590
Yes. CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

18:14.590 --> 18:19.960
Well, clearly the fiscal cliff is having effects on the economy.

18:19.960 --> 18:22.290
Even though we've not yet even reached the point

18:22.290 --> 18:26.250
of the fiscal cliff potentially kicking in,

18:26.250 --> 18:29.710
it's already affecting business investment and hiring decisions

18:29.710 --> 18:32.850
by creating uncertainty or creating pessimism.

18:32.850 --> 18:37.280
We saw what happened recently to consumer sentiment, which fell,

18:37.280 --> 18:40.890
presumably in part because of concerns about the fiscal cliff.

18:40.890 --> 18:46.080
So, clearly, this is a major risk factor and a major source of uncertainty

18:46.080 --> 18:49.480
about the economy going forward.

18:49.480 --> 18:55.820
I would suspect and, although the participants don't all make this explicit,

18:55.820 --> 18:59.190
but I would suspect that what they are assuming in their projections is

18:59.190 --> 19:03.370
that the fiscal cliff gets resolved in some intermediate way,

19:03.370 --> 19:07.180
whereby there is still some fiscal drag but not as much as implied

19:07.180 --> 19:09.730
by the entire fiscal cliff.

19:09.730 --> 19:13.900
So I think that's probably the underlying assumption that most people took

19:13.900 --> 19:16.280
when they made their projections.

19:16.280 --> 19:19.720
But as you-you're absolutely right that there is a lot of uncertainty right now

19:19.720 --> 19:24.610
and if the fiscal cliff situation turns out to be resolved

19:24.610 --> 19:26.520
in a way very different from our expectations,

19:26.520 --> 19:30.280
I'm sure you would see changes in the forecast.

19:30.280 --> 19:35.330
ALISTER BULL.

19:35.330 --> 19:36.400
Alister Bull from Reuters.

19:36.400 --> 19:38.510
Thanks very much, Mr. Chairman.

19:38.510 --> 19:44.270
Could you talk about whether the decision to maintain the monthly bond purchases

19:44.270 --> 19:48.620
at 85 billion a month represents a ramping up or additional easing

19:48.620 --> 19:51.730
to Fed policy, because you're now going to be adding a little bit more

19:51.730 --> 19:53.700
to the size of balance sheet?

19:53.700 --> 20:00.430
And also, you know, you've talked about maintaining the asset purchases

20:00.430 --> 20:03.750
until you see a substantial improvement in the outlook for the labor market,

20:03.750 --> 20:06.440
and you've said that you want to take a qualitative approach,

20:06.440 --> 20:11.600
but you've also got this 6½ percent inflation threshold out there as well.

20:11.600 --> 20:14.240
So, could you talk about what sort of evidence you'd see to sort

20:14.240 --> 20:17.730
of make you change the pace or slow the pace of purchases?

20:17.730 --> 20:18.510
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

20:18.510 --> 20:21.010
The first part of your question was?

20:21.010 --> 20:21.290
ALISTER BULL.

20:21.290 --> 20:25.230
Is this an additional stimulus or are you- CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

20:25.230 --> 20:28.800
No, I think this is really a continuation of what we said in September.

20:28.800 --> 20:33.300
You recall in September we expressed dissatisfaction

20:33.300 --> 20:35.170
with progress in the labor market.

20:35.170 --> 20:39.500
We, at that point, we began the $40 billion per month of MBS purchases

20:39.500 --> 20:44.110
and we said that unless we saw substantial improvement in the outlook

20:44.110 --> 20:47.360
for the labor market that we would undertake additional asset purchases

20:47.360 --> 20:49.150
or other actions.

20:49.150 --> 20:52.340
And that's what we've done today is simply follow

20:52.340 --> 20:55.260
through on what we said we would do in September.

20:55.260 --> 20:58.310
I don't think that we have, relative to last month,

20:58.310 --> 21:03.300
I don't think we have significantly added to accommodation.

21:03.300 --> 21:08.310
The reason is that, at least in my view and I think many of my colleagues',

21:08.310 --> 21:13.080
that what matters primarily is the mix of assets on the balance sheet,

21:13.080 --> 21:15.700
on the asset side of the balance sheet.

21:15.700 --> 21:22.100
So what's important is the fact that we're acquiring Treasury securities

21:22.100 --> 21:25.010
and MBS, taking those out of the market, you know,

21:25.010 --> 21:27.920
forcing investors into other closely related assets

21:27.920 --> 21:29.390
and that's where the stimulus comes from,

21:29.390 --> 21:32.220
not so much in the size of the balance sheet per se.

21:32.220 --> 21:37.090
So, in my judgment, the amount of stimulus is more

21:37.090 --> 21:38.930
or less the same, it's just being continued.

21:38.930 --> 21:43.510
It's a follow-through from what we saw in September.

21:43.510 --> 21:45.890
In terms of criteria, again,

21:45.890 --> 21:50.300
what we've done is we've announced an initial amount

21:50.300 --> 21:54.360
of 85 billion a month of purchases.

21:54.360 --> 21:57.370
We are prepared to vary that as new information comes in.

21:57.370 --> 22:03.080
For example, if the economy's outlook gets noticeably stronger,

22:03.080 --> 22:07.490
we would presumably begin to ramp down the level of purchases.

22:07.490 --> 22:14.100
But again, the problem with giving a specific number is

22:14.100 --> 22:18.450
that there are multiple criteria on which we make this decision.

22:18.450 --> 22:21.780
We'll be looking at the outlook for labor market, which is very important,

22:21.780 --> 22:24.120
but we'll also be looking at other factors

22:24.120 --> 22:26.260
that may be affecting the outlook for the economy.

22:26.260 --> 22:31.840
For example, I hope it won't happen but if the fiscal cliff occurs,

22:31.840 --> 22:36.120
as I've said many times, I don't think the Federal Reserve has the tools

22:36.120 --> 22:39.040
to offset that event and, in that case,

22:39.040 --> 22:42.840
we obviously have to temper our expectations about what we can accomplish.

22:42.840 --> 22:45.570
Likewise, as I said, we'll also be looking at the efficacy

22:45.570 --> 22:50.680
and costs of our program and if, you know, we find,

22:50.680 --> 22:53.830
we expect it to be efficacious but if we find that it's not working as well

22:53.830 --> 22:56.800
as we hoped or if the various costs are emerging

22:56.800 --> 22:59.380
that we had not anticipated then that would also have to be taken

22:59.380 --> 23:00.690
into account as we think about it.

23:00.690 --> 23:02.510
So, we thought it was not constructive

23:02.510 --> 23:06.520
because we ourselves don't know precisely what would define

23:06.520 --> 23:07.700
substantial improvement.

23:07.700 --> 23:14.550
But obviously, as long as the costs and other concerns do not emerge,

23:14.550 --> 23:16.340
we'll be looking for, you know,

23:16.340 --> 23:22.780
something that is substantial in terms of a better job market.

23:22.780 --> 23:24.860
PETER COOK.

23:24.860 --> 23:29.110
Peter Cook with Bloomberg Television.

23:29.110 --> 23:32.530
Mr. Chairman, if I could follow up just on your last response there.

23:32.530 --> 23:35.650
Given the fiscal cliff, is it possible that if the country,

23:35.650 --> 23:39.470
if the policy makers were not to agree to some sort of deficit deal by the end

23:39.470 --> 23:42.060
of this year and we were to go over the fiscal cliff,

23:42.060 --> 23:46.260
that the size of these asset purchases could indeed grow in response to that?

23:46.260 --> 23:49.020
And more specifically, you coined the phrase "fiscal cliff,"

23:49.020 --> 23:50.090
and I wanted to get your take

23:50.090 --> 23:52.990
on whether you still feel it's the most appropriate language

23:52.990 --> 23:55.430
to describe what would happen at the beginning of the year.

23:55.430 --> 23:58.720
There are some Americans who may be alarmed by the language,

23:58.720 --> 24:00.410
some economists say it's more of a slope.

24:00.410 --> 24:04.240
Do you still feel it's appropriate given the circumstances- the contraction,

24:04.240 --> 24:06.590
the fiscal contraction, that would come if there is no deal?

24:06.590 --> 24:07.790
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

24:07.790 --> 24:11.280
Well, for the first part of your question

24:11.280 --> 24:15.540
if the economy actually went off the fiscal cliff, our assessment,

24:15.540 --> 24:17.640
the CBO's assessment, outside forecasters,

24:17.640 --> 24:21.810
all think that that would have very significant adverse effects on the economy

24:21.810 --> 24:23.160
and on the unemployment rate.

24:23.160 --> 24:26.100
And so, on the margin, we would try to do what we could.

24:26.100 --> 24:29.450
We would perhaps increase a bit.

24:29.450 --> 24:31.510
But I just want to, again, be clear

24:31.510 --> 24:38.610
that we cannot offset the full impact of the fiscal cliff.

24:38.610 --> 24:43.200
It's just too big given the tools that we have available and the limitations

24:43.200 --> 24:46.860
on our policy toolkit at this point.

24:46.860 --> 24:52.530
On terms of the terminology, well, and people have different preferences

24:52.530 --> 24:54.220
about what they want to call things,

24:54.220 --> 24:58.250
I think it's a sensible term because I think

24:58.250 --> 25:01.830
of the fiscal policies providing support to the economy.

25:01.830 --> 25:04.690
If fiscal policy becomes very contractionary,

25:04.690 --> 25:07.200
the economy will, I think, go off a cliff.

25:07.200 --> 25:10.660
I think that it's reasonable to be concerned about this.

25:10.660 --> 25:19.460
I don't buy the idea that a short-term descent off the fiscal cliff would be

25:19.460 --> 25:20.850
not costly.

25:20.850 --> 25:25.370
I think it would be costly and, in fact, we're already seeing costs.

25:25.370 --> 25:28.880
Why is it that consumer confidence dropped so sharply this week?

25:28.880 --> 25:32.210
Why is it that small business confidence dropped so sharply?

25:32.210 --> 25:34.720
Why is-why are the markets volatile?

25:34.720 --> 25:41.540
Why is business investment at among its weakest levels during the recovery?

25:41.540 --> 25:43.540
I think all of these things, at least to some extent,

25:43.540 --> 25:47.620
can be traced to the anticipation and the concern about the fiscal cliff

25:47.620 --> 25:51.410
and I think that, you know, we don't know exactly what would happen

25:51.410 --> 25:57.590
but I think there is certainly a risk that it could be serious and, therefore,

25:57.590 --> 25:59.370
I think it is very important.

25:59.370 --> 26:02.100
The most helpful thing that could-that, I think, Congress,

26:02.100 --> 26:05.240
the Administration could do right now is find a resolution that,

26:05.240 --> 26:09.200
on the one hand, achieves long run fiscal sustainability, which is critical,

26:09.200 --> 26:12.070
absolutely critical for a healthy economy,

26:12.070 --> 26:19.040
but also avoids derailing the recovery which is currently in process.

26:19.040 --> 26:20.960
JON HILSENRATH.

26:24.860 --> 26:26.710
Jon Hilsenrath from the Wall Street Journal.

26:26.710 --> 26:30.940
Mr. Chairman, I just-I want to try to square up some numbers.

26:30.940 --> 26:32.720
The threshold that you announced today

26:32.720 --> 26:36.090
for when rate hikes might start is 6½ percent.

26:36.090 --> 26:40.980
The Committee's assessment of the longer-run unemployment rate is 6 percent,

26:40.980 --> 26:46.800
or perhaps a bit lower, and the longer-run equilibrium fed funds rate is

26:46.800 --> 26:49.830
around 4 percent according to these projections.

26:49.830 --> 26:54.080
It suggests that when the Fed does start raising interest rates down the road,

26:54.080 --> 26:58.870
it might have to move fairly quickly to get to some equilibrium fed funds rate.

26:58.870 --> 27:03.350
Is that, specifically, is that the case and, more generally,

27:03.350 --> 27:07.540
can you talk about what this framework that you set up today says

27:07.540 --> 27:10.410
about the exit strategy that you had laid out some time ago

27:10.410 --> 27:12.850
and whether that's evolving or changing?

27:12.850 --> 27:14.370
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

27:14.370 --> 27:15.250
Well, it's a good question.

27:15.250 --> 27:19.080
So first of all, we don't have a precise estimate

27:19.080 --> 27:22.620
of the long-run sustainable unemployment rate.

27:22.620 --> 27:29.490
The estimates that were provided in the Summary of Economic Projections today,

27:29.490 --> 27:35.050
as has been the case for a while, is 5.2 to 6.0 percent.

27:35.050 --> 27:42.380
So, it could be well less than 6½ percent, so that gives us some time.

27:42.380 --> 27:50.640
My anticipation is that the removal of accommodation after the takeoff point,

27:50.640 --> 27:53.900
whenever that occurs, would be relatively gradual.

27:53.900 --> 27:57.040
I don't think we're looking at a rapid increase.

27:57.040 --> 28:00.390
Of course, that depends on where inflation is and other conditions.

28:00.390 --> 28:07.990
But it's-the path that we're basing these numbers on is one that assumes,

28:07.990 --> 28:12.310
first of all, as you anticipated,

28:12.310 --> 28:16.000
assumes an increase in the funds rate first occurring some time

28:16.000 --> 28:19.060
after unemployment goes below 6½ percent,

28:19.060 --> 28:22.950
but does not necessarily assume a rapid increase after that.

28:22.950 --> 28:26.580
And I-what we said in our statement is that we would take a balanced approach.

28:26.580 --> 28:30.020
In other words, you know, once we get to that point,

28:30.020 --> 28:32.140
we may or may not raise rates at that point.

28:32.140 --> 28:34.380
We'll look at the situation.

28:34.380 --> 28:40.230
But assuming that inflation remains well controlled, which I fully anticipate,

28:40.230 --> 28:45.920
I think that the rate of increase in rates would be moderate.

28:45.920 --> 28:54.610
This is consistent-the exit strategy that we put out is consistent

28:54.610 --> 28:59.150
with our statement today because the exit strategy was primarily

28:59.150 --> 29:02.540
about how we would normalize the balance sheet over time.

29:02.540 --> 29:04.750
And at this time we have not made any changes in that,

29:04.750 --> 29:08.740
and we believe that some increase in the size

29:08.740 --> 29:14.940
of our balance sheet is consistent still with that general sequence that we laid

29:14.940 --> 29:17.430
out in the minutes a year and a half ago.

29:17.430 --> 29:20.660
That being said, if the balance sheet grows by enough,

29:20.660 --> 29:24.110
we may have to reconsider the pace or timing of that,

29:24.110 --> 29:28.320
but I don't see any changes that would, you know,

29:28.320 --> 29:34.120
radically change the time to normalization or the time to exit.

29:34.120 --> 29:38.210
ZACHARY GOLDFARB.

29:38.210 --> 29:38.550
Thank you.

29:38.550 --> 29:39.160
Zach Goldfarb.

29:39.160 --> 29:43.140
I'm going to continue the two question trend and ask-first on the fiscal cliff,

29:43.140 --> 29:46.610
it sounds like you would prefer a sensible balance of fiscal consolidation

29:46.610 --> 29:50.240
and support for the recovery, but if people in Congress can't do

29:50.240 --> 29:53.970
that in the next two weeks, do you think postponing off this whole consolidation

29:53.970 --> 29:56.930
is preferable to going over the cliff?

29:56.930 --> 29:59.950
And then, on the monetary policy actions today,

29:59.950 --> 30:01.470
can you give us a little bit more color

30:01.470 --> 30:04.300
on how you set the thresholds at what they were?

30:04.300 --> 30:05.910
And what the alternatives were,

30:05.910 --> 30:09.740
and how you weighed various alternative but similar policies?

30:09.740 --> 30:10.900
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

30:10.900 --> 30:16.500
Sure. I'm hoping that the Congress will do the right thing on the fiscal cliff.

30:16.500 --> 30:21.690
I-you know, there's a problem with kicking the can down the road.

30:21.690 --> 30:24.460
It might avoid some of the short-term impact on the recovery,

30:24.460 --> 30:29.600
but it could create concerns about our longer term fiscal situation

30:29.600 --> 30:31.360
and I don't want to see that.

30:31.360 --> 30:36.220
So I think for the best-it's in the best interest of the economy to come

30:36.220 --> 30:39.340
to a two-part solution, if you will.

30:39.340 --> 30:43.630
Part one is to modify fiscal policy in a way

30:43.630 --> 30:48.870
that doesn't create enormous headwinds for the recovery in the near term.

30:48.870 --> 30:57.400
And part two is to at least take important steps towards achieving a framework,

30:57.400 --> 31:01.800
at least, by which, perhaps through further negotiation,

31:01.800 --> 31:07.380
the Congress and the Administration can achieve a sustainable path

31:07.380 --> 31:08.240
for fiscal policy.

31:08.240 --> 31:10.280
That-both of these parts are very important.

31:10.280 --> 31:14.870
I don't think that we can consider these negotiations a success unless both

31:14.870 --> 31:16.320
of them happen.

31:16.320 --> 31:19.210
[Speaker unknown; inaudible remark] CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

31:19.210 --> 31:21.910
No. I think they're equally important.

31:21.910 --> 31:28.300
On the threshold numbers, this is based-these numbers are based

31:28.300 --> 31:34.590
on substantial analysis done by staff, both here and at the Reserve Banks,

31:34.590 --> 31:40.280
trying to assess under so-called optimal policy,

31:40.280 --> 31:43.590
or in the best policy that we can come up with,

31:43.590 --> 31:48.560
what would the interest rate path look like and how would it be connected

31:48.560 --> 31:51.980
or correlated with changes in unemployment and inflation.

31:51.980 --> 31:54.130
And when we do that analysis,

31:54.130 --> 32:00.360
what we find is that the best interest rate path-as best

32:00.360 --> 32:03.230
as we can determine it based on our models and analysis,

32:03.230 --> 32:08.110
which is obviously imperfect-has rates remaining low

32:08.110 --> 32:11.190
until unemployment drops below 6½ percent.

32:11.190 --> 32:15.100
And it projects if we put in the two-the half of a percentage point

32:15.100 --> 32:19.950
above the 2 percent goal as a kind of protection against any problem

32:19.950 --> 32:21.980
with price stability, but our projections,

32:21.980 --> 32:25.170
our actual forecast suggests that inflation will not go there.

32:25.170 --> 32:26.890
Inflation will stay around 2 percent,

32:26.890 --> 32:29.900
which will be consistent with our longer-term objective.

32:29.900 --> 32:30.960
ZACH GOLDFARB.

32:30.960 --> 32:33.060
[Inaudible Remark] CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

32:33.060 --> 32:37.360
If we got important new information about the structure of the economy,

32:37.360 --> 32:42.750
it's possible, but I consider it relatively unlikely and this is one

32:42.750 --> 32:46.170
of the best-this is one of the advantages of this approach

32:46.170 --> 32:48.130
over the date-based approach.

32:48.130 --> 32:50.720
If information comes in which says the economy is stronger

32:50.720 --> 32:53.400
or weaker than we expected, that would, in principle,

32:53.400 --> 32:55.580
require a change in the date.

32:55.580 --> 32:58.420
But it doesn't necessarily require a change in the thresholds

32:58.420 --> 33:04.000
because that date adjustment can be made by markets just simply by looking

33:04.000 --> 33:07.640
at their own forecasts of when unemployment will cross the line

33:07.640 --> 33:09.710
and the behavior of inflation.

33:09.710 --> 33:12.310
SCOTT SPOERRY.

33:14.390 --> 33:19.220
Scott Spoerry from CNN, sir.

33:19.220 --> 33:23.730
When you were appearing on 60 Minutes, one of the things that happened was

33:23.730 --> 33:26.260
that you visited your old hometown,

33:26.260 --> 33:31.350
and you talked a little bit about how the economy had affected people

33:31.350 --> 33:36.570
that you grew up with and affected the people down there.

33:36.570 --> 33:40.240
There are a lot of just regular people like that who are

33:40.240 --> 33:44.640
out on the countryside wondering what really happens to them

33:44.640 --> 33:46.710
if we do go over the federal cliff.

33:46.710 --> 33:50.460
Taxes go up, spending goes down.

33:50.460 --> 33:52.600
Do they need to look for a recession?

33:52.600 --> 33:57.810
Are employers going to really cut back on the employment, do you think?

33:57.810 --> 34:01.580
What do people out there really need to worry about

34:01.580 --> 34:07.080
and prepare for when it comes to actually going over that fiscal cliff,

34:07.080 --> 34:10.660
if the folks in Washington can't get their act together?

34:10.660 --> 34:11.830
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

34:11.830 --> 34:14.810
Well, I come from a part of South Carolina

34:14.810 --> 34:18.940
which has been economically challenged for quite a long time and remains so.

34:18.940 --> 34:22.410
I mean certain parts of South Carolina have developed pretty strongly

34:22.410 --> 34:26.110
but the part where I come from-mostly agricultural,

34:26.110 --> 34:28.980
it has a little bit of manufacturing-has a very high unemployment rate,

34:28.980 --> 34:33.100
a high foreclosure rate, and people are having a hard time there.

34:33.100 --> 34:37.490
And I've visited there a few times since I became Chairman.

34:37.490 --> 34:44.140
So, part of the reason that, you know, we are engaging these policies is to try

34:44.140 --> 34:51.370
and create a stronger economy-more jobs-so that folks across the country,

34:51.370 --> 34:55.740
including places like the one where I grew up, you know,

34:55.740 --> 34:59.750
will have more opportunity to have better lives for themselves.

34:59.750 --> 35:02.440
So that's extremely important and I think it's very important

35:02.440 --> 35:06.180
that we not just look at the numbers.

35:06.180 --> 35:08.330
It's easy to look at the unemployment rate and say,

35:08.330 --> 35:10.640
well it's one-tenth or two-tenths.

35:10.640 --> 35:16.300
Every tenth means, you know, many, many people are represented there.

35:16.300 --> 35:21.660
So it is very important to try to keep in mind the reality of unemployment,

35:21.660 --> 35:26.840
of foreclosure, of weak wage growth, et cetera, so we always try to do that.

35:26.840 --> 35:33.570
And I want to-it's always a delicate balance-you don't want to scare people.

35:33.570 --> 35:38.490
And I actually believe that Congress will come up with a solution,

35:38.490 --> 35:41.100
and I certainly hope they will.

35:41.100 --> 35:47.400
But as many analysts, not just the Fed, have pointed out,

35:47.400 --> 35:51.590
if the fiscal cliff was allowed to occur, and certainly if it were sustained

35:51.590 --> 35:57.520
for any period, it could have a very negative effect on hiring, jobs, wages,

35:57.520 --> 36:01.800
economic activity, investment.

36:01.800 --> 36:06.700
And of course, the consequences of that will be felt by everybody but certainly

36:06.700 --> 36:14.130
by those in areas like where I grew up that are relatively weak economically,

36:14.130 --> 36:16.650
would no doubt would feel the greater brunt.

36:16.650 --> 36:21.250
So, it's exceptionally urgent and important that the Congress

36:21.250 --> 36:25.220
and the Administration come to a sensible agreement on this issue.

36:25.220 --> 36:25.530
SCOTT SPOERRY.

36:25.530 --> 36:26.900
Okay, sir, I had a follow-up, too.

36:26.900 --> 36:29.910
I'm not going to ask you whether we're in a bond bubble.

36:29.910 --> 36:37.230
But obviously, the new guidance that you've given in the FOMC statement is going

36:37.230 --> 36:42.410
to give a lot more clues to people who own bonds

36:42.410 --> 36:48.540
about when they might start lightening up their bond portfolios

36:48.540 --> 36:52.310
and changing the composition of what they own.

36:52.310 --> 36:59.350
Was concerns about information about bond prices and things happening

36:59.350 --> 37:05.410
in a big hurry, in terms of some sort of a bubble popping-was

37:05.410 --> 37:09.190
that a consideration in adding this transparency?

37:09.190 --> 37:09.730
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

37:09.730 --> 37:13.250
I wouldn't say it was an important motivation for adding transparency.

37:13.250 --> 37:15.570
I think transparency has a lot of value.

37:15.570 --> 37:22.180
But it is the-it is a fact that this greater clarity will help markets better

37:22.180 --> 37:25.170
predict how bond yields will behave.

37:25.170 --> 37:31.740
As we go forward in time, if the economy continues to strengthen as we hope,

37:31.740 --> 37:34.540
as the exit comes closer for the Federal Reserve,

37:34.540 --> 37:38.030
then you would expect longer-term bond yields to begin to rise.

37:38.030 --> 37:44.010
And the more information we can provide to markets about the conditionality

37:44.010 --> 37:47.860
under which the Fed would consider removing accommodation,

37:47.860 --> 37:51.260
the better information they'll have about the likely path of bonds

37:51.260 --> 37:53.660
and that will allow for a smoother adjustment.

37:53.660 --> 37:56.750
So I think that's a positive aspect of this communication.

37:56.750 --> 37:59.050
It-I wouldn't say it was the major reason.

37:59.050 --> 38:05.910
The major reason is to give the markets and the public more transparency

38:05.910 --> 38:10.690
about what's determining our policy, but that is one potential advantage.

38:10.690 --> 38:13.100
ROBIN HARDING.

38:13.100 --> 38:20.570
Robin Harding from the Financial Times.

38:20.570 --> 38:23.780
Mr. Chairman, you said a moment ago that these thresholds are based

38:23.780 --> 38:25.770
on an analysis of optimal policy.

38:25.770 --> 38:30.080
In the optimal policy path that Vice Chair Yellen laid

38:30.080 --> 38:32.560
out in her speech a few weeks ago,

38:32.560 --> 38:37.110
it showed the first rise in interest rates occurring in early 2016

38:37.110 --> 38:39.910
and then rates rising very slowly after that.

38:39.910 --> 38:42.310
Is that the policy that the Fed is now following?

38:42.310 --> 38:47.200
And secondly, if I may, you referred to a number of inflation forecasts

38:47.200 --> 38:49.500
in your introductory remarks.

38:49.500 --> 38:53.820
In that case, how will we ever know that the inflation threshold has been hit?

38:53.820 --> 38:54.600
Thank you.

38:54.600 --> 38:56.120
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

38:56.120 --> 39:02.890
So, the kind of optimal policy path

39:02.890 --> 39:06.220
that Vice Chairman Yellen showed is indicative

39:06.220 --> 39:07.730
of the kinds of analysis we've done.

39:07.730 --> 39:10.370
I mean, we've run it for a variety of different scenarios,

39:10.370 --> 39:12.220
different assumptions about models, and so on,

39:12.220 --> 39:17.620
but the general character of that interest rate path, i.e.,

39:17.620 --> 39:26.390
that it stays low until unemployment is in the vicinity of 6½ or a little lower,

39:26.390 --> 39:30.100
and then rises relatively slowly, which goes back to the question

39:30.100 --> 39:35.570
that was asked earlier, that it doesn't involve a rapid removal of accommodation

39:35.570 --> 39:37.740
after that point is reached.

39:37.740 --> 39:43.400
That is consistent with that kind of analysis, and that's the type of analysis

39:43.400 --> 39:48.070
that was not the only thing we looked at but was informative in our discussion.

39:48.070 --> 39:53.690
You'll note also in that kind of policy path, of the type that she discussed,

39:53.690 --> 39:58.260
that inflation stays essentially at 2 or very close to 2.

39:58.260 --> 40:00.730
In terms of inflation forecast,

40:00.730 --> 40:07.220
the-what the Committee will do on a regular basis is include

40:07.220 --> 40:13.500
in its statement its views of where inflation is likely to be a year from now.

40:13.500 --> 40:16.080
For example, currently, we already say that, you know,

40:16.080 --> 40:17.550
we expect inflation to run at

40:17.550 --> 40:22.380
or below the Committee's objective in a longer term.

40:22.380 --> 40:25.350
The intellectual exercise we'll be doing is asking ourselves

40:25.350 --> 40:30.060
if we maintain low rates along the lines suggested by this policy,

40:30.060 --> 40:37.120
would we expect inflation to cross the threshold or reach that level?

40:37.120 --> 40:42.750
Now, it's very important that the public, the media,

40:42.750 --> 40:47.780
the markets find our projections credible, obviously.

40:47.780 --> 40:52.070
And so for that reason, you know, we will be referring extensively

40:52.070 --> 40:55.660
to publicly available information such as various measures

40:55.660 --> 41:00.590
of inflation-I mentioned some in my opening remarks-outside forecasts,

41:00.590 --> 41:06.890
the breakevens from inflation protected bonds, et cetera.

41:06.890 --> 41:12.680
So, if our outlook deviates in any sense in a significant way from sort

41:12.680 --> 41:16.710
of what all these things are saying, at a minimum, it will be incumbent on me

41:16.710 --> 41:19.200
and the rest of us to explain that.

41:19.200 --> 41:25.920
But my expectation is that our projections will be broadly consistent

41:25.920 --> 41:28.970
with public views, public information,

41:28.970 --> 41:32.360
and so I think we can manage the credibility issue.

41:32.360 --> 41:33.970
But again, just to be clear,

41:33.970 --> 41:38.150
the projection that matters for our determination is the one

41:38.150 --> 41:43.280
that the Committee collectively comes up with.

41:43.280 --> 41:45.750
BINYAMIN APPLEBAUM.

41:45.750 --> 41:48.890
Sir, you've articulated more clearly than ever your commitment

41:48.890 --> 41:51.680
to reduce unemployment, but you've also said

41:51.680 --> 41:53.900
that you're not actually doing anything more to achieve

41:53.900 --> 41:57.080
that goal-that you still expect it to be three years away,

41:57.080 --> 41:59.520
that you're disappointed with the pace of progress,

41:59.520 --> 42:02.300
and that inflation is not the limiting factor.

42:02.300 --> 42:03.740
What is the limiting factor?

42:03.740 --> 42:07.800
Why is the Fed not announcing today additional measures to reduce unemployment?

42:07.800 --> 42:11.110
What would it take for you to get that?

42:11.110 --> 42:11.470
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

42:11.470 --> 42:15.660
Well, we took-the question was whether this was something new relative

42:15.660 --> 42:17.970
to September, I think September was the date

42:17.970 --> 42:22.150
where we did do a substantial increase in accommodation.

42:22.150 --> 42:25.580
At that point, we announced our dissatisfaction with the state

42:25.580 --> 42:28.310
of the labor market and the outlook for jobs

42:28.310 --> 42:34.270
and said we would take further action if the outlook didn't improve.

42:34.270 --> 42:38.550
And what we've done today is really just following through what we said.

42:38.550 --> 42:43.480
So, I would say that, you know, looking at it from the perspective of September,

42:43.480 --> 42:50.900
that we have, in fact, taken significant additional action to provide support

42:50.900 --> 42:53.560
for the recovery and for job creation.

42:53.560 --> 42:58.130
The reason-one of the considerations though, as I've talked about, is that,

42:58.130 --> 43:02.550
you know, given that we are now in the world of unconventional policy

43:02.550 --> 43:10.010
that has both uncertain costs and uncertain efficacy or uncertain benefits,

43:10.010 --> 43:14.910
you know, that creates a more-somewhat more complicated policy decision

43:14.910 --> 43:18.310
than the old style of just changing the federal funds rate.

43:18.310 --> 43:26.250
You know, there are concerns that I've talked about in these briefings before

43:26.250 --> 43:30.760
that if the balance sheet gets indefinitely large

43:30.760 --> 43:34.060
that there would be potential risks in terms of financial stability,

43:34.060 --> 43:35.960
in terms of market functioning.

43:35.960 --> 43:40.610
And the Committee takes these risks very seriously.

43:40.610 --> 43:46.760
And they impose a certain cost on policy that doesn't exist

43:46.760 --> 43:48.840
when you're dealing only with the federal funds rate.

43:48.840 --> 43:54.290
And so what we're trying to do here is balance the potential benefits,

43:54.290 --> 43:58.770
in terms of lower unemployment and inflation at target,

43:58.770 --> 44:05.690
against the reality that as the balance sheet gets bigger

44:05.690 --> 44:09.930
that there's greater costs that might be associated with that,

44:09.930 --> 44:11.540
and those have to be taken to account.

44:11.540 --> 44:12.380
BINYAMIN APPLEBAUM.

44:12.380 --> 44:16.680
Sir, if I can just follow, so these forecasts presumably incorporate the actions

44:16.680 --> 44:21.060
taken into account, and so given those actions,

44:21.060 --> 44:24.070
it will still be three years until you achieve your goals.

44:24.070 --> 44:26.800
Is the message to people who are unemployed basically,

44:26.800 --> 44:28.250
"We are doing all we can?"

44:28.250 --> 44:31.610
Is this the conclusion that, given that balance of factors,

44:31.610 --> 44:32.840
this is the most we can expect?

44:32.840 --> 44:33.230
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

44:33.230 --> 44:37.280
Well, first of all, again, these-the projections that you're looking

44:37.280 --> 44:41.640
at are based on each individual-this is not a Committee collective projection.

44:41.640 --> 44:45.330
What they are, of course, is 19 separate participants making their own

44:45.330 --> 44:50.960
projections based on their own views of optimal policy.

44:50.960 --> 44:54.770
So, for example, it includes those folks who think

44:54.770 --> 44:57.360
that we shouldn't be doing any more purchases

44:57.360 --> 45:01.390
and their forecasts are included in there as well.

45:01.390 --> 45:05.600
So, it's not exactly an apples to apples comparison,

45:05.600 --> 45:10.290
but it is true that if we could wave a magic wand and get unemployment

45:10.290 --> 45:13.160
down to 5 percent tomorrow, obviously we would do that,

45:13.160 --> 45:18.670
but there are constraints in terms of the dynamics of the economy,

45:18.670 --> 45:26.040
in terms of the power of these tools, and in terms of the fact that we do need

45:26.040 --> 45:31.000
to take into account, you know, the possibility of other costs

45:31.000 --> 45:41.600
and risks that might be associated with a large expansion of our balance sheet.

45:41.600 --> 45:42.100
DARREN GERSH.

45:42.100 --> 45:44.090
To just-Darren Gersh, Nightly Business Report.

45:44.090 --> 45:49.280
So just following up on that last question, how helpful would it be to see,

45:49.280 --> 45:53.310
as part of the fiscal cliff resolution, some near-term stimulus?

45:53.310 --> 45:54.760
The President's proposed that.

45:54.760 --> 45:57.020
Do you think-how helpful do you think that would be and,

45:57.020 --> 46:00.290
I'll ask my follow up now, whatever happened to your southern accent?

46:00.290 --> 46:02.700
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

46:02.700 --> 46:07.570
Well, on the second one, I'd like to think I'm bilingual.

46:07.570 --> 46:10.830
When I go home sometimes I-it comes out pretty strongly,

46:10.830 --> 46:13.880
but I won't try to do that here.

46:13.880 --> 46:18.710
So, I try to be careful, as you know,

46:18.710 --> 46:23.910
not to give views on specific tax and spending programs.

46:23.910 --> 46:27.180
I think, of course, obviously those are the province

46:27.180 --> 46:29.540
of the Administration and Congress.

46:29.540 --> 46:33.580
The attitude I've taken has been that, at a minimum,

46:33.580 --> 46:39.590
Congress should try to do no harm, that they should try to avoid policies

46:39.590 --> 46:44.620
that significantly slow or derail the recovery at this point.

46:44.620 --> 46:47.960
So I think that's the critical thing along with the long-term objective

46:47.960 --> 46:50.560
of achieving a sustainable fiscal path.

46:50.560 --> 46:57.850
Now, given that basic recommendation, you know,

46:57.850 --> 47:00.530
Congress can consider variations.

47:00.530 --> 47:03.490
For example, if they believe that they can achieve a strong,

47:03.490 --> 47:07.860
credible future path for fiscal policy,

47:07.860 --> 47:09.780
that would give them potentially some space

47:09.780 --> 47:15.510
to do something a little bit more expansionary in the short term.

47:15.510 --> 47:18.100
But, those are judgments I think that Congress has to make

47:18.100 --> 47:25.540
about whether they can simultaneously continue supportive fiscal policy

47:25.540 --> 47:30.050
in the short term while maintaining the credibility that, in fact,

47:30.050 --> 47:33.200
they will be addressing our structural deficit problems in the longer term.

47:33.200 --> 47:39.130
And that is I think really a question for them and for their staff.

47:39.130 --> 47:39.980
KRISTINA PETERSON.

47:39.980 --> 47:45.610
Kristina Peterson of Dow Jones.

47:45.610 --> 47:50.550
Looking over the past year or several years,

47:50.550 --> 47:54.320
how would you evaluate the Fed's accuracy making economic forecasts,

47:54.320 --> 47:58.370
and how does that affect the ability to make monetary policy decisions,

47:58.370 --> 48:01.010
especially as it's connected to the thresholds?

48:01.010 --> 48:02.020
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

48:02.020 --> 48:10.170
Well, I think it's fair to say that we have overestimated the pace of growth,

48:10.170 --> 48:17.370
total output growth, GDP growth, from the beginning of the recovery

48:17.370 --> 48:19.990
and we have had therefore-had to continue to scale

48:19.990 --> 48:24.770
down our estimates of output growth.

48:24.770 --> 48:29.130
But interestingly, at the same time, we have been more accurate,

48:29.130 --> 48:32.440
not perfectly accurate by any means, but we've been somewhat more accurate

48:32.440 --> 48:34.560
in forecasting unemployment.

48:34.560 --> 48:36.610
And how do you reconcile those two things?

48:36.610 --> 48:38.330
I talked about this in remarks I gave

48:38.330 --> 48:42.180
at the New York Economic Club recently, right before Thanksgiving.

48:42.180 --> 48:46.610
And I think the reconciliation is that what we're learning is that,

48:46.610 --> 48:52.480
at least temporarily, the financial crisis may have reduced somewhat the

48:52.480 --> 48:55.950
underlying potential growth rate of the U.S. economy.

48:55.950 --> 48:59.050
It has interfered with business creation, with investment,

48:59.050 --> 49:02.240
with technological advances, and so on.

49:02.240 --> 49:08.680
And that can account for at least part of the somewhat slower growth.

49:08.680 --> 49:11.060
At the same time, though, what-of course,

49:11.060 --> 49:15.160
what monetary policy influences is not potential growth,

49:15.160 --> 49:16.920
not the underlying structural growth.

49:16.920 --> 49:20.940
That's for-many other different kinds of policies affect that.

49:20.940 --> 49:25.560
What monetary policy affects, primarily, is the state of the business cycle,

49:25.560 --> 49:33.800
the amount of excess unemployment or the extent of recession in the economy.

49:33.800 --> 49:39.880
And there, I think we've also perhaps underestimated a bit the recession,

49:39.880 --> 49:42.110
but we've been much closer there.

49:42.110 --> 49:48.190
And I think therefore that we've been able to address

49:48.190 --> 49:52.750
that somewhat more effectively with quite accommodative policies.

49:52.750 --> 49:56.940
That being said, of course, we have over time,

49:56.940 --> 50:03.910
as we have seen disappointment in growth and job creation, we have obviously,

50:03.910 --> 50:07.280
as we did in September, have added accommodation

50:07.280 --> 50:12.800
and we've continued-we continue to reassess the outlook.

50:12.800 --> 50:14.940
I think it's only fair to say

50:14.940 --> 50:20.370
that economic forecasting beyond a few quarters is very, very difficult.

50:20.370 --> 50:26.000
And what we basically are trying to do is create a plausible scenario

50:26.000 --> 50:29.210
which we think is reasonably likely-base policy on that,

50:29.210 --> 50:32.530
but we prepared to adjust as new information comes in

50:32.530 --> 50:36.540
and as the outlook changes, and inevitably it will.

50:36.540 --> 50:44.950
GREG IP. Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

50:44.950 --> 50:46.030
Greg Ip of the Economist.

50:46.030 --> 50:47.740
Economists have long believed

50:47.740 --> 50:51.240
that central banks cannot affect the unemployment rate in the long run.

50:51.240 --> 50:54.620
That's one reason you've seen a move towards central banks being given mandates

50:54.620 --> 50:55.980
for low inflation only.

50:55.980 --> 51:00.020
Can you explain if the Fed, by tying its monetary policy so explicitly

51:00.020 --> 51:02.640
to an unemployment threshold, whether that is consistent or inconsistent

51:02.640 --> 51:04.020
with that longstanding view?

51:04.020 --> 51:06.590
And, if it's consistent, how is this superior

51:06.590 --> 51:09.250
to simply having a threshold for inflation only?

51:09.250 --> 51:12.090
And would the approach that you're now taking be possible

51:12.090 --> 51:15.250
if the Fed had only a mandate for low inflation?

51:15.250 --> 51:16.250
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

51:16.250 --> 51:21.460
Well, it's entirely consistent with your view, with the point that you made.

51:21.460 --> 51:25.900
So let me just reiterate it.

51:25.900 --> 51:31.640
As we stated, in fact, in our January set of principles,

51:31.640 --> 51:37.860
the central bank cannot control unemployment in the long run.

51:37.860 --> 51:38.960
I'd add a caveat to this.

51:38.960 --> 51:44.550
There's a little bit of a caveat here, which is that very extended periods

51:44.550 --> 51:49.340
of unemployment can interfere with the workings of the labor market.

51:49.340 --> 51:55.320
And so, if the Fed were not to address a large unemployment problem

51:55.320 --> 51:56.730
for a long time, it might, in fact,

51:56.730 --> 51:59.300
have some influence on the long term unemployment rate.

51:59.300 --> 52:01.350
But as a general rule, as a general rule,

52:01.350 --> 52:03.320
and I think this is the right baseline,

52:03.320 --> 52:11.130
the long-term unemployment rate is determined by a range of structural features

52:11.130 --> 52:16.720
of the economy and a range of economic policies and not by monetary policy.

52:16.720 --> 52:25.340
So that being said, what our 6½ percent threshold is is-as I said

52:25.340 --> 52:31.220
in my opening remarks, it is not a target-what it is is a guidepost in terms

52:31.220 --> 52:40.640
of when the beginning of the reduction of accommodation could begin.

52:40.640 --> 52:42.080
It could be later than that,

52:42.080 --> 52:48.220
but at least by that time, no earlier than that time.

52:48.220 --> 52:53.830
So it's really more like a reaction function or a Taylor rule, if you will.

52:53.830 --> 52:57.280
I don't want-I'm-I'll get-I'm ready to get the phone call from John Taylor.

52:57.280 --> 53:02.190
It is not a Taylor rule but it has the same feature that it relates policy

53:02.190 --> 53:09.350
to observables in the economy, such as unemployment and inflation.

53:09.350 --> 53:12.290
So, what it's basically doing is saying how our policy will evolve

53:12.290 --> 53:14.200
over time as the economy evolves.

53:14.200 --> 53:18.950
It has no implication that we can affect the long-run unemployment rate,

53:18.950 --> 53:21.580
which we believe is lower than 6½ percent.

53:21.580 --> 53:26.470
We think it's somewhere between 5 and 6 according to our SEP projections.

53:26.470 --> 53:32.490
We are a dual mandate central bank and I think that providing information

53:32.490 --> 53:37.060
on both sides is more helpful.

53:37.060 --> 53:41.800
So, I understand your point but I think that it's-that providing information

53:41.800 --> 53:47.190
on unemployment and inflation gives more information to the markets,

53:47.190 --> 53:54.920
to the public that allows them to infer how our policy is likely to evolve.

53:54.920 --> 53:59.580
[Speaker unknown; inaudible remark] CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

53:59.580 --> 54:04.010
Unless-so long as the inflation condition is met.

54:04.010 --> 54:06.270
It's correct.

54:06.270 --> 54:08.510
WYATT ANDREWS.

54:08.510 --> 54:11.080
Mr. Chairman, Wyatt Andrews, CBS News.

54:11.080 --> 54:12.880
I'd like-still like to hear a little bit more

54:12.880 --> 54:18.310
about why you made this announcement today specifically tying federal funds

54:18.310 --> 54:21.370
and your policy to the 6.5 percent number?

54:21.370 --> 54:25.610
I'm sure you have some sort of theory about what you hope changes in the economy

54:25.610 --> 54:27.580
as a result of this announcement.

54:27.580 --> 54:29.510
If so, what is it?

54:29.510 --> 54:30.910
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

54:30.910 --> 54:33.210
Well, we think it's a better form of communication.

54:33.210 --> 54:39.970
We think it's-by using the thresholds which ties rates to economic conditions,

54:39.970 --> 54:46.230
we're more transparent about what's going to determine our policy in the future.

54:46.230 --> 54:50.810
The date-based guidance, it served a purpose.

54:50.810 --> 54:55.560
But it had the problem that whenever economic outlook changed,

54:55.560 --> 54:58.000
the Committee was faced with the question

54:58.000 --> 54:59.680
of whether we should change the date-based guidance,

54:59.680 --> 55:02.320
and we did change it a couple of times.

55:02.320 --> 55:04.500
But that was a nontransparent process.

55:04.500 --> 55:09.420
Nobody understood exactly why we made a particular change

55:09.420 --> 55:11.920
because we were not providing any kind of fundamental information

55:11.920 --> 55:14.900
about how our policy is linked to the underlying outlook.

55:14.900 --> 55:19.900
And so, we, I believe, certainly, that this approach is superior.

55:19.900 --> 55:21.540
I'm not saying it's the best possible approach,

55:21.540 --> 55:23.210
there may be other things we can do in the future.

55:23.210 --> 55:26.090
We're always looking to find ways to improve our communication

55:26.090 --> 55:31.540
but I do think it's more transparent and will allow the markets

55:31.540 --> 55:36.020
to respond quickly and promptly to changes in the outlook by adjusting

55:36.020 --> 55:40.150
when they think rate increases will begin, and therefore, it'll act,

55:40.150 --> 55:41.920
to some extent, like an automatic stabilizer.

55:41.920 --> 55:47.740
So if the outlook worsens and that leads markets to think that the increase

55:47.740 --> 55:52.400
in rates is further out in the future, that will tend to lower longer term rates

55:52.400 --> 55:54.670
and that would tend to be supportive of the economy.

55:54.670 --> 55:56.770
So that has an automatic stabilizer type effect.

55:56.770 --> 55:58.920
It kind of offsets adverse shocks.

55:58.920 --> 56:00.820
So it's a better form of communication.

56:00.820 --> 56:04.110
As I said, we discussed it quite extensively at the last meeting.

56:04.110 --> 56:13.280
And so-and frankly, given that it's a relatively complex change,

56:13.280 --> 56:16.720
it seemed like it would be a good idea to do it at a meeting

56:16.720 --> 56:18.470
where there was a press conference.

56:18.470 --> 56:25.640
So, we decided since we're ready to go why not make the change earlier

56:25.640 --> 56:26.850
and get the benefit earlier.

56:26.850 --> 56:27.300
WYATT ANDREWS.

56:27.300 --> 56:30.690
As a quick follow up, are you-did you see a level of uncertainty

56:30.690 --> 56:34.100
in the business community that you hope to solve by this announcement?

56:34.100 --> 56:35.600
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

56:35.600 --> 56:42.830
Well, at the moment, I think that the expectations of the business community

56:42.830 --> 56:49.000
and of the Federal Open Market Committee happen to be pretty well aligned.

56:49.000 --> 56:52.260
If you look at the financial market indicators,

56:52.260 --> 56:55.410
the future federal funds rate path,

56:55.410 --> 56:59.710
it's pretty consistent with the mid-2015 date-based guidance

56:59.710 --> 57:00.670
that we had been providing.

57:00.670 --> 57:02.760
So I'm not saying there was a major inconsistency

57:02.760 --> 57:06.610
between what the business community was expecting, the markets were expecting,

57:06.610 --> 57:08.200
and what we were expecting.

57:08.200 --> 57:09.340
So that wasn't really the issue.

57:09.340 --> 57:12.750
The problem is that, looking forward,

57:12.750 --> 57:17.540
that what happens if there is a significant change either for the better,

57:17.540 --> 57:20.210
or for the worse, in the outlook?

57:20.210 --> 57:24.750
Under the date-based guidance, that would require the Committee to determine,

57:24.750 --> 57:29.760
you know, what the new date is and to make that change in a nontransparent way.

57:29.760 --> 57:33.170
But under this threshold-based guidance,

57:33.170 --> 57:38.540
the markets and the business community can make that calculation on their own

57:38.540 --> 57:46.770
and adjust that-their estimates of when rates will begin to increase based only

57:46.770 --> 57:48.850
on their own forecast and not have to wait

57:48.850 --> 57:52.070
for the Federal Open Market Committee to give them a date.

57:52.070 --> 57:57.850
So we just think it's a better approach.

57:57.850 --> 58:00.540
JOSH ZUMBRUN.

58:00.540 --> 58:02.860
Mr. Chairman, Josh Zumbrun, Bloomberg News.

58:02.860 --> 58:06.380
By mid-2015, the recovery is going to be nearly six years old.

58:06.380 --> 58:10.090
The average postwar recovery has been a little less than five.

58:10.090 --> 58:13.250
We're already banking on a very long expansion.

58:13.250 --> 58:16.710
You expect, by mid-2015, the funds rate is going to be at zero percent,

58:16.710 --> 58:19.740
your balance sheet is potentially at $4 trillion.

58:19.740 --> 58:22.460
If the business cycle runs out of steam and you're still

58:22.460 --> 58:24.310
at zero percent interest rates,

58:24.310 --> 58:28.640
does the Fed no longer have a forceful response in that situation?

58:28.640 --> 58:30.070
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

58:30.070 --> 58:35.850
Well, the Fed will always, you know, keep-we've innovated quite a bit

58:35.850 --> 58:39.370
in the last few years and it's always possible we could find new ways

58:39.370 --> 58:41.480
to provide support for the economy.

58:41.480 --> 58:44.230
But it's certainly true, I've-you know,

58:44.230 --> 58:48.160
there's no doubt that with interest rates near zero

58:48.160 --> 58:51.640
and with the balance sheet already large,

58:51.640 --> 58:55.650
that the ability to provide additional accommodation is not unlimited

58:55.650 --> 58:59.400
and that that's just a reality and that actually is an argument, I think,

58:59.400 --> 59:00.960
for being a little bit more aggressive now.

59:00.960 --> 59:05.910
I mean, it's a really good objective to get the economy moving,

59:05.910 --> 59:10.900
to get some momentum that protects the economy against unanticipated shocks

59:10.900 --> 59:15.060
that might occur, and gets us off the zero bound earlier.

59:15.060 --> 59:18.640
So, exactly for those reasons,

59:18.640 --> 59:23.270
the kind of risks that arise when the-when policy interest rates are close

59:23.270 --> 59:28.100
to zero, and the greater difficulty in providing additional policy support,

59:28.100 --> 59:32.490
I think that's an argument for being somewhat more proactive now,

59:32.490 --> 59:37.410
when we still have the ability to do that, and to try to get the economy,

59:37.410 --> 59:40.160
you know, back to a healthy condition.

59:40.160 --> 59:41.050
DONNA BORAK.

59:41.050 --> 59:41.750
Hi, Chairman.

59:41.750 --> 59:44.050
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

59:44.050 --> 59:45.480
Hi. DONNA BORAK.

59:45.480 --> 59:48.130
Donna Borak with American Banker.

59:48.130 --> 59:50.310
My question pertains to the Volcker Rule.

59:50.310 --> 59:53.880
Regulators, earlier this year, seemed cautiously optimistic that they'd be able

59:53.880 --> 59:56.160
to finalize a rule by the end of the year.

59:56.160 --> 59:58.820
However, that seems a bit unlikely at this point.

59:58.820 --> 01:00:00.910
And now, as you know, lawmakers are calling

01:00:00.910 --> 01:00:03.980
for a two-year implementation delay on the rule.

01:00:03.980 --> 01:00:06.340
Given the fact that it's been such a lengthy process,

01:00:06.340 --> 01:00:09.190
can you tell us where things stand at this point?

01:00:09.190 --> 01:00:11.860
How much closer are we to a final rule?

01:00:11.860 --> 01:00:15.070
Have the agencies been able to work out their differences?

01:00:15.070 --> 01:00:18.440
And, if you may, a prediction on when we might actually see a rule.

01:00:18.440 --> 01:00:19.630
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

01:00:19.630 --> 01:00:21.020
We've made quite a bit of progress.

01:00:21.020 --> 01:00:26.460
It's a difficult, complex rule, as you know, and we've had, I think I recall,

01:00:26.460 --> 01:00:29.240
18,000 comments or something like that.

01:00:29.240 --> 01:00:31.550
So it's been a lot to look at.

01:00:31.550 --> 01:00:35.800
There are a lot of concerns that arose even from, you know,

01:00:35.800 --> 01:00:40.110
foreign commenters about the effects on their bond markets, et cetera.

01:00:40.110 --> 01:00:42.750
So there's been a lot of work to do.

01:00:42.750 --> 01:00:45.090
There, I think there is quite a bit of agreement,

01:00:45.090 --> 01:00:46.270
I wouldn't say a final agreement,

01:00:46.270 --> 01:00:47.450
but quite a bit of agreement

01:00:47.450 --> 01:00:51.610
on key points among the regulators, at this juncture.

01:00:51.610 --> 01:00:56.620
And, of course, if Congress gives us some other instruction, we'll follow that,

01:00:56.620 --> 01:00:59.450
but so far, we haven't received any different instruction.

01:00:59.450 --> 01:01:05.420
So it's our intent to try to get this done early in 2013.

01:01:05.420 --> 01:01:08.520
PETER BARNES.

01:01:08.520 --> 01:01:10.970
Peter Barnes, Fox Business, Mr. Chairman.

01:01:10.970 --> 01:01:14.450
Since your last press conference with us, we've had an election.

01:01:14.450 --> 01:01:17.060
One of the candidates in that election, Governor Romney,

01:01:17.060 --> 01:01:21.210
said that he would not reappoint you to a third term as Chairman.

01:01:21.210 --> 01:01:26.340
President Obama did not weigh in on the issue but he was-he did win reelection.

01:01:26.340 --> 01:01:33.090
If the President were to call you and say, "Ben,

01:01:33.090 --> 01:01:38.260
your country needs your continued stewardship at the Federal Reserve.

01:01:38.260 --> 01:01:43.680
We need you to stay and finish the job, see this through."

01:01:43.680 --> 01:01:45.090
Would you consider it?

01:01:45.090 --> 01:01:49.610
Would you do it, and, by chance, have you had any conversations to that effect

01:01:49.610 --> 01:01:52.090
with the President or anybody on his team?

01:01:52.090 --> 01:01:52.270
Thank you.

01:01:52.270 --> 01:01:53.730
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

01:01:53.730 --> 01:01:56.980
So to answer the last part, no, I haven't had any conversations.

01:01:56.980 --> 01:02:01.790
I think the President has got quite a few issues he needs to be thinking about,

01:02:01.790 --> 01:02:04.800
from fiscal cliff to many other appointments and so on.

01:02:04.800 --> 01:02:09.760
From my own perspective, I just, I really don't have anything to add

01:02:09.760 --> 01:02:11.990
from the last press conference.

01:02:11.990 --> 01:02:16.150
I am very much engaged in these difficult issues that we're discussing today,

01:02:16.150 --> 01:02:21.270
and I have not been spending time thinking about my own future.

01:02:21.270 --> 01:02:29.740
So I don't really have anything to add there.

01:02:29.740 --> 01:02:30.480
KEVIN HALL.

01:02:30.480 --> 01:02:32.940
Okay, Mr. Chairman.

01:02:32.940 --> 01:02:33.490
Thank you.

01:02:33.490 --> 01:02:37.790
The two questions, taking the Goldfarb rule into effect,

01:02:37.790 --> 01:02:39.190
one having to do with the CPI,

01:02:39.190 --> 01:02:42.180
there's a lot of debate now with the Boehner plan about this chained CPI.

01:02:42.180 --> 01:02:44.070
As an econ-not as a Fed Chairman

01:02:44.070 --> 01:02:46.870
but as an economist-is there a logic in going to that?

01:02:46.870 --> 01:02:52.480
Do you think it's a move that should be done separate from the politics of it?

01:02:52.480 --> 01:02:55.230
And again, with your economist hat, the labor market,

01:02:55.230 --> 01:02:58.550
you talked about the importance, not looking at the 6.5 percent threshold,

01:02:58.550 --> 01:03:00.260
but the broader conditions.

01:03:00.260 --> 01:03:03.420
A big debate-Steve Liesman's nemesis talks a lot

01:03:03.420 --> 01:03:05.210
about people being beamed to Mars.

01:03:05.210 --> 01:03:07.700
In your mind, what is happening in the job market?

01:03:07.700 --> 01:03:09.330
Are we creating jobs?

01:03:09.330 --> 01:03:10.830
Is that why it's coming down

01:03:10.830 --> 01:03:13.590
or is it because people-the degree of discouraged workers?

01:03:13.590 --> 01:03:19.930
What is your sense of how quickly it's fallen because of new employment?

01:03:19.930 --> 01:03:21.550
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

01:03:21.550 --> 01:03:24.920
So, on the first question, the chained CPI

01:03:24.920 --> 01:03:29.080
versus the fixed weight CPI is a technical issue.

01:03:29.080 --> 01:03:32.940
The chained CPI is technically better, according to most economists,

01:03:32.940 --> 01:03:36.530
because it allows for changes in the mix of goods

01:03:36.530 --> 01:03:41.410
and services that people actually consume more effectively.

01:03:41.410 --> 01:03:47.320
However, whether, you know, whether that's more appropriate for, say,

01:03:47.320 --> 01:03:51.880
Social Security indexing or not, I think that's ultimately a political decision.

01:03:51.880 --> 01:03:54.420
I suppose a rejoinder would be

01:03:54.420 --> 01:03:58.660
that neither the CPI nor the chained CPI may necessarily be a particularly good

01:03:58.660 --> 01:04:02.290
measure of the cost of living for Social Security recipients.

01:04:02.290 --> 01:04:04.240
So, those are the kinds of questions

01:04:04.240 --> 01:04:10.640
that Congress is going to have to deal with.

01:04:10.640 --> 01:04:13.120
Second part of your question was?

01:04:13.120 --> 01:04:14.420
KEVIN HALL.

01:04:14.420 --> 01:04:18.740
The debate over the extent to which the unemployment rate is falling from-

01:04:18.740 --> 01:04:19.310
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

01:04:19.310 --> 01:04:19.720
Ah, yes. Sorry.

01:04:19.720 --> 01:04:20.130
KEVIN HALL.

01:04:20.130 --> 01:04:21.820
New jobs versus people leaving.

01:04:21.820 --> 01:04:22.190
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

01:04:22.190 --> 01:04:29.460
Yes. Well, you know, you can see the comparison by looking, for example,

01:04:29.460 --> 01:04:37.370
at the household survey, which gives estimates of how many people are added

01:04:37.370 --> 01:04:40.600
to the labor force, how many are added to the employed,

01:04:40.600 --> 01:04:42.540
how many people are leaving the labor force,

01:04:42.540 --> 01:04:46.480
and it's true that part of the decline in unemployment-and indeed all of it

01:04:46.480 --> 01:04:50.070
in the last reading, but over the recovery,

01:04:50.070 --> 01:04:54.330
part of the decline in unemployment has come from declines

01:04:54.330 --> 01:04:58.330
in participation rates, that is, people leaving the labor force.

01:04:58.330 --> 01:05:05.610
Some of that decline in participation appears to be due to longer-run factors,

01:05:05.610 --> 01:05:10.690
aging and changing patterns of work among women.

01:05:10.690 --> 01:05:14.240
So those things were probably not directly related

01:05:14.240 --> 01:05:17.680
to the recession, for example.

01:05:17.680 --> 01:05:20.580
But beyond that downward trend, there's been some additional decline

01:05:20.580 --> 01:05:25.300
in labor force participation, and in the ratio of employment to population,

01:05:25.300 --> 01:05:32.520
which presumably is linked to discouragement about the state of the labor force.

01:05:32.520 --> 01:05:35.330
So that certainly is part of the issue.

01:05:35.330 --> 01:05:39.040
That being said, obviously there has been a good bit of job creation.

01:05:39.040 --> 01:05:41.810
You can see that either in the household survey

01:05:41.810 --> 01:05:47.310
or in the payroll establishment survey.

01:05:47.310 --> 01:05:54.760
So I think there's no doubt that the labor market is considerably better today

01:05:54.760 --> 01:05:59.290
than it was two years ago, there's not any question about that.

01:05:59.290 --> 01:06:02.630
But it's also the case that many indicators

01:06:02.630 --> 01:06:06.580
of the labor market remain quite weak,

01:06:06.580 --> 01:06:09.800
ranging from the number of long-term unemployed to the number of people

01:06:09.800 --> 01:06:13.870
who have part-time work who would like full-time work.

01:06:13.870 --> 01:06:22.670
Wage growth obviously is very weak, and I could go on.

01:06:22.670 --> 01:06:28.230
So, it may be that the labor market is even a bit weaker

01:06:28.230 --> 01:06:30.010
than the current unemployment rate suggests,

01:06:30.010 --> 01:06:35.170
but I think that it is nevertheless the case that there has been improvement

01:06:35.170 --> 01:06:38.890
since the trough a couple of years ago.

01:06:38.890 --> 01:06:42.240
CATHERINE HOLLANDER.

01:06:43.350 --> 01:06:46.260
Mr. Chairman, Catherine Hollander from National Journal.

01:06:46.260 --> 01:06:49.870
How concerned are you that markets will have to tank in order to get lawmakers

01:06:49.870 --> 01:06:51.770
to reach a deal on the fiscal cliff,

01:06:51.770 --> 01:06:54.130
and what do you make of their recent complacency?

01:06:54.130 --> 01:06:56.340
Is there a Wall Street-Washington disconnect?

01:06:56.340 --> 01:06:57.720
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

01:06:57.720 --> 01:06:59.970
Interesting question.

01:06:59.970 --> 01:07:02.550
Well, I certainly hope that markets won't have to tank.

01:07:02.550 --> 01:07:09.030
I don't-we want to have confidence, not just in markets but in businesses

01:07:09.030 --> 01:07:15.760
and households as well, and the best way the fiscal policymakers can achieve

01:07:15.760 --> 01:07:24.950
that is by coming to a solution as quickly as possible.

01:07:25.990 --> 01:07:29.840
Markets have obviously already responded to some extent, up and down;

01:07:29.840 --> 01:07:36.140
you can see from day to day how they respond to news about the negotiations.

01:07:36.140 --> 01:07:40.390
But on the other hand, it's also true, if you look at the experience,

01:07:40.390 --> 01:07:45.270
I think the very informative experience of the debt limit debate in August

01:07:45.270 --> 01:07:55.680
of 2011, that both confidence and markets remain pretty sanguine

01:07:55.680 --> 01:07:58.230
up to pretty close to the point where it looked

01:07:58.230 --> 01:08:04.030
like there was actually a chance the debt limit would not be raised.

01:08:04.030 --> 01:08:09.570
And then, of course, there was a pretty sharp shock, particularly to confidence,

01:08:09.570 --> 01:08:18.970
about the time of the, you know, of the final debates.

01:08:18.970 --> 01:08:25.810
So it's not unusual to see markets being complacent.

01:08:25.810 --> 01:08:29.320
Of course, there's from a market point of view,

01:08:29.320 --> 01:08:32.080
there are sort of risks in both directions.

01:08:32.080 --> 01:08:35.400
Obviously, things go badly, but perhaps if things go especially well,

01:08:35.400 --> 01:08:38.960
that would be good news, and maybe right now markets are sort

01:08:38.960 --> 01:08:41.450
of taking an average of those two possibilities.

01:08:41.450 --> 01:08:48.560
But again, just to reiterate, I don't think any policymaker, including the Fed,

01:08:48.560 --> 01:08:51.670
should be responding to markets.

01:08:51.670 --> 01:08:58.570
What we should be doing is making policy, you know, based on the fundamentals

01:08:58.570 --> 01:09:01.090
and doing what's best for the economy

01:09:01.090 --> 01:09:10.940
and I hope that fiscal policymakers will follow that injunction as well.

01:09:10.940 --> 01:09:14.700
STEVE BECKNER.

01:09:14.700 --> 01:09:18.120
Mr. Chairman, Steve Beckner of Market News International.

01:09:18.120 --> 01:09:22.860
With the federal government borrowing roughly $1 trillion a year

01:09:22.860 --> 01:09:31.040
and now with the Fed on pace to buy roughly a trillion dollars a year in bonds,

01:09:31.040 --> 01:09:34.920
are you concerned about a public, and possibly global,

01:09:34.920 --> 01:09:39.400
perception that the Fed is accommodating not just growth

01:09:39.400 --> 01:09:42.030
but accommodating federal borrowing needs,

01:09:42.030 --> 01:09:46.840
and are you concerned about what this might do to the Fed's credibility,

01:09:46.840 --> 01:09:50.580
and the credibility of U.S. finances in general,

01:09:50.580 --> 01:09:54.120
and the credibility of the dollar as the world's leading currency?

01:09:54.120 --> 01:09:55.700
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

01:09:55.700 --> 01:10:00.430
Well, first of all, just a couple of facts.

01:10:00.430 --> 01:10:05.400
The-we're buying Treasuries and mortgage-backed securities,

01:10:05.400 --> 01:10:08.030
about half and half, roughly.

01:10:08.030 --> 01:10:15.080
So we're buying considerably less than the Treasury is issuing and, moreover,

01:10:15.080 --> 01:10:18.870
the share of outstanding Treasuries that the Federal Reserve owns is not all

01:10:18.870 --> 01:10:21.180
that different from what it was before the crisis,

01:10:21.180 --> 01:10:23.090
because while our holdings have increased,

01:10:23.090 --> 01:10:27.550
so has the-obviously the stock of Treasuries in public hands.

01:10:27.550 --> 01:10:32.680
So it's not quite evident that there has been such a radical shift there.

01:10:32.680 --> 01:10:36.240
You know, we've been increasing our balance sheet now for some time,

01:10:36.240 --> 01:10:41.370
and we've been very clear that this is a temporary measure.

01:10:41.370 --> 01:10:44.960
It's a way to provide additional accommodation

01:10:44.960 --> 01:10:47.150
to an economy which needs support.

01:10:47.150 --> 01:10:50.420
We've been equally clear that we will normalize the balance sheet,

01:10:50.420 --> 01:10:55.730
that we will reduce the size of our holdings and-whether by letting them run off

01:10:55.730 --> 01:11:00.230
or by selling assets in the future.

01:11:00.230 --> 01:11:02.550
So this is, again, only a temporary step.

01:11:02.550 --> 01:11:10.960
It would be quite a different matter if we were buying these assets

01:11:10.960 --> 01:11:12.080
and holding them indefinitely.

01:11:12.080 --> 01:11:14.900
That would be monetization.

01:11:14.900 --> 01:11:15.880
We're not doing that.

01:11:15.880 --> 01:11:18.360
We are very clear about our intentions.

01:11:18.360 --> 01:11:25.080
And I think up until now, it seems our credibility has been quite good.

01:11:25.080 --> 01:11:28.740
There is not any sign either of current inflation

01:11:28.740 --> 01:11:33.240
or of any-there's no strong evidence that there are any increases

01:11:33.240 --> 01:11:36.680
in inflation expectations for that matter, looking at financial markets,

01:11:36.680 --> 01:11:41.650
looking at surveys, looking at economic forecasts, and so on.

01:11:41.650 --> 01:11:44.420
So, this is one of the things that we have to look at.

01:11:44.420 --> 01:11:48.540
Remember, I talked earlier about the potential costs of a large balance sheet.

01:11:48.540 --> 01:11:52.640
We want to be sure that there's no misunderstanding,

01:11:52.640 --> 01:11:55.440
that there's no effect on inflation expectations

01:11:55.440 --> 01:11:56.480
from the size of our balance sheet.

01:11:56.480 --> 01:12:02.090
That's one of the things we have to look at, but as to this point,

01:12:02.090 --> 01:12:06.420
there just really is no evidence that people are taking it that way.

01:12:06.420 --> 01:12:09.660
And I guess it's worth pointing out-of course we've been very focused

01:12:09.660 --> 01:12:13.780
on the United States here, but we're not the only central bank

01:12:13.780 --> 01:12:15.990
that has increased the size of its balance sheet.

01:12:15.990 --> 01:12:20.700
The Japanese, the Europeans, the British have all done the same,

01:12:20.700 --> 01:12:25.570
and very much more or less to the same extent in terms of the fraction of GDP,

01:12:25.570 --> 01:12:31.710
and I think the sophisticated market players and the public understand

01:12:31.710 --> 01:12:35.780
that this is part of a collective need,

01:12:35.780 --> 01:12:40.130
a need to provide additional accommodation to weak economies

01:12:40.130 --> 01:12:45.000
and not an accommodation of fiscal policy.

01:12:45.000 --> 01:12:46.940
GREG ROBB.

01:12:50.070 --> 01:12:53.770
Last but not least, Greg Robb, MarketWatch.

01:12:53.770 --> 01:12:58.410
There seems to be growing evidence that some of the MBS purchases,

01:12:58.410 --> 01:13:02.950
the impact of the MBS purchases, are that banks are holding on to some

01:13:02.950 --> 01:13:06.080
of the gains and not passing them on to the borrowers.

01:13:06.080 --> 01:13:09.310
Is there anything you can do about that and are you concerned about that?

01:13:09.310 --> 01:13:09.870
Thank you.

01:13:09.870 --> 01:13:10.600
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

01:13:10.600 --> 01:13:16.360
So the question is-just to restate your question-is about the spread

01:13:16.360 --> 01:13:20.580
between the mortgage rates that the public pays and the yields

01:13:20.580 --> 01:13:25.900
to mortgage-backed securities that banks may hold, and the question is,

01:13:25.900 --> 01:13:32.730
is that spread widening so that the full benefit of the reduction

01:13:32.730 --> 01:13:35.860
in MBS yields is not being passed through, that's the question.

01:13:35.860 --> 01:13:43.080
Well, I have to make sure everybody heard it, so you could answer.

01:13:43.080 --> 01:13:51.480
Our analysis suggests that it takes time-two points.

01:13:51.480 --> 01:13:57.510
The first point is that while we don't expect a hundred percent pass-through

01:13:57.510 --> 01:14:00.760
of MBS yields to mortgage rates,

01:14:00.760 --> 01:14:06.070
our empirical and theoretical analysis-and we've had quite a bit of work done

01:14:06.070 --> 01:14:11.970
on this issue-suggests that over time, the great majority of the decline

01:14:11.970 --> 01:14:17.520
in MBS yields does get passed through to mortgage rates.

01:14:17.520 --> 01:14:24.320
So we do anticipate, over time, that the full benefit will,

01:14:24.320 --> 01:14:28.370
or most of the benefit will, be seen by retail customers.

01:14:28.370 --> 01:14:31.300
And indeed, we have seen already a pretty significant decline

01:14:31.300 --> 01:14:34.840
since September in retail mortgage rates.

01:14:34.840 --> 01:14:40.310
But one thing that's perhaps confusing this issue a little bit is

01:14:40.310 --> 01:14:42.330
that there are other things happening in the economy

01:14:42.330 --> 01:14:44.920
which are affecting those spreads.

01:14:44.920 --> 01:14:53.530
So, for example, there are capacity limitations, which are allowing banks to,

01:14:53.530 --> 01:14:58.640
you know, to charge higher yields, there's extra costs like concerns

01:14:58.640 --> 01:15:01.000
about putback risk, for example.

01:15:01.000 --> 01:15:02.310
There's higher g-fees.

01:15:02.310 --> 01:15:06.210
So there's a number of things happening in the economy which will tend,

01:15:06.210 --> 01:15:10.810
all else equal, to raise that spread between mortgage rates

01:15:10.810 --> 01:15:14.070
and MBS yields, so that's unfortunate.

01:15:14.070 --> 01:15:19.730
What we can try to do there, of course, is to try to encourage good policy,

01:15:19.730 --> 01:15:23.520
for example, on putbacks, that will reduce the perceived risk

01:15:23.520 --> 01:15:28.170
and cost to the banks of making mortgage loans.

01:15:28.170 --> 01:15:34.180
But again, you know, I think most of those things, like g-fees, for example,

01:15:34.180 --> 01:15:40.640
are not really in our control, but, again, taking all those issues as constant,

01:15:40.640 --> 01:15:44.590
it does seem to be the case that, over a period of time, not immediately,

01:15:44.590 --> 01:15:48.730
but over a period of time, most of the declines in MBS yields do find their way

01:15:48.730 --> 01:15:57.480
through to mortgage customers and thereby strengthen the housing market.

